Abstract
               Solvation behaviour of some copper(I) perchlorate complexes has been investigated in binary mixtures of acetonitrile (AN) and dimethylsulphoxide (DMSO) containing 1.0, 0.8, 0.6, 0.4, 0.2 and 0 mol fraction of AN at 298 K by molar conductances and viscosity measurements. The conductance data have been analyzed by using the Shedlovsky method to calculate limiting molar conductance (Λ
                  o
                  ) and the viscosity data by the Jones–Dole equation to evaluate viscosity B-coefficients of various salts. Limiting ion conductances (λ
                  i
                  o
                  ), solvated radii (ri
                  ) and ionic viscosity B
                  ± coefficients in all solvent systems have been evaluated using Gill´s modification based on Bu4NBPh4 as a reference electrolyte. The studies show that all copper(I) complexes are solvated and the extent of solvation is stronger in AN and AN rich regions than in DMSO and DMSO rich regions. The ClO4
                  - ion is poorly solvated in AN+DMSO mixtures. The viscosity results are in good agreement with the results obtained from the conductance studies.

To understand the solute-solute and solute-solvent interactions between the ionic liquids (ILs) and molecular solvent, in this work, we have measured thermophysical properties of pyrrolidinium based ILs in acetonitrile solutions. Also, the influence of IL cation chain length and temperature were quantified through derived volumetric and acoustic parameters. These derived properties were obtained from the measurement of the density (ρ), and speed of sound (u) for binary solutions of two ILs, i.e. 1-ethyl-1-methyl pyrrolidinium iodide and 1-methyl-1-propyl pyrrolidinium iodide with acetonitrile in the concentration range from 0.05 to 0.4mol·kg−1 and the temperature range from 293.15 to 328.15K at atmospheric pressure. Further, the Redlich-Mayer type of equation was fitted to these derived properties, apparent molar volume (V

                        ϕ
                     ) and apparent molar isentropic compression (K

                        s,ϕ
                     ), in order to evaluate the type and extent of interactions occurring between the studied ILs and acetonitrile.

